{"t":1793429982,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"8b897104-34d4-4ca1-ba87-0a2972cc74f7","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-31T06:48:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 310648\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n248 AM EDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nSeasonable temperatures and dry conditions for Halloween. No \nhazardous weather expected through Tuesday with precipitation \nchances returning by the middle and end of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Seasonable temperatures and dry conditions continue through\n  Tuesday. Precipitation chances return late next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable temperatures and dry conditions continue through \nTuesday. Precipitation chances return late next week. \n\n1033-1035 mb surface high pressure will build in from the north \ntoday before meandering over the Chesapeake Bay/Delmarva Sunday into \nMonday. As a result, expect light surface winds out of the north and \nnorthwest today switching to the south Sunday. Temperatures will \nremain seasonable throughout the weekend with highs in the 60s (50s \nmountains) and lows in the 40s (30s valleys).\n\nHigh pressure will gradually move offshore Tuesday into Wednesday as \na deep upper level trough approaches from the Tennessee/lower Ohio \nRiver Valleys. Guidance continues to show numerous pieces of \nshortwave energy expected to traverse the base of this trough during \nthis period. Additionally, there is indication that a cutoff low \nwill develop over the southeastern CONUS ahead of this trough \npattern. The interaction of these systems is likely to be complex, \nwith it being hard to tell exactly where all of the pieces line up \nat this stage.The GFS has at least two cutoff lows along the trough \nitself while the Canadian and Euro have a low along the trough and \noff the coastal Carolinas. As for timing, the GFS and Canadian are \nslower compared to the Euro counterpart. With that said, expect dry \nconditions to continue through Wednesday as high pressure retreats \nfurther offshore allowing the ridge to flatten/mid-level dry air to \nerode. This will allow precipitation chances to increase from the \nsouthwest late Wednesday with widespread rainfall Thursday into \nFriday. High pressure will quickly return behind the system Friday \ninto the upcoming weekend before a more potent trough/push of cold \nair follows beyond the period. \n\nMonday through Thursday: High temperatures will be in the upper 60s \nto low 70s east of the Blue Ridge with those along and west in the \nupper 50s to 60s. Overnight low temperatures will be in the 50s each \nnight.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /06Z SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nPrevailing VFR conditions are expected through at least Tuesday. \nHigh pressure will build from the north today before meandering over \nthe area Sunday into Monday and pushing offshore by the middle of \nnext week. Winds will be light and variable out of the north today \nbefore switching to the south Sunday and southeast Monday at less \nthan 10 kts. Some patchy fog or frost is possible in the river \nvalleys over the next few mornings with ground fog at the local\ncorridor terminals prior to daybreak each morning. Confidence \nin this is low given ample dry air in place across the region. \nHowever, some low chances of sub-VFR ceilings exist late Monday \nnight into Tuesday depending on how the weather pattern evolves.\n\nSub-VFR conditions may return as early as Wednesday as rain chances \nreturn to the forecast. More widespread sub-VFR conditions are \nexpected THursday into Friday as a wave of low pressure crosses the \narea. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSCA conditions (north/northwest gusts 20-25 kts) are expected \nthrough early this morning, especially over the open waters. This is \nlargely due in part to northerly channeling. Winds diminish below \nbelow sub-SCA levels this afternoon. Sub-SCA conditions look to \ncontinue Sunday into Monday with wedging high pressure meandering \nnearby. Winds will change to the south and southeast as high \npressure sits north and east of the area. Speeds will generally be \nbetween 5-10 kts.\n\nWinds turn out of the east Tuesday into Wednesday ahead of an \napproaching low pressure system, but should remain short of SCA \ncriteria until at least Wednesday evening. Thereafter, winds do pick \nup slightly and could reach criteria Wednesday night. \n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory until 9 AM EDT this morning for MDZ008.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 9 AM EDT this morning for \n     ANZ530>543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...CJL/KRR/EST\nAVIATION...CJL/EST\nMARINE...CJL/EST\n"},"forecast":{"t":1793429940,"days":{"2026-10-31":{"hi":64,"lo":46,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-01":{"hi":66,"lo":45,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-11-02":{"hi":67,"lo":46,"pop":0,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-03":{"hi":72,"lo":53,"pop":0,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-04":{"hi":73,"lo":58,"pop":42,"short":"Partly Sunny then Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-11-05":{"hi":73,"lo":58,"pop":57,"short":"Showers And Thunderstorms Likely"},"2026-11-06":{"hi":71,"lo":null,"pop":52,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1793426400,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,22700,20800,null,17100,21600,21600,17800,17800,17800,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[5,5,4,4,4,4,5,5,6,6,6,5,4,4,4,3,3,3,